-- title:
--   An unramified DVR subring A₀ with A=A₀[ζ_q]
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime with $q\ge 5$, let $M'$ be a non-zero natural number with $q\nmid M'$, and let $\ell$ be a prime with $\ell\ge 3$ and $\ell\neq q$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of level $\{q\ell\}$, and let $\zeta\in L$ be a primitive $q$-th root of unity. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a domain, equipped with an algebra structure over which $L$ is its fraction field, assume $q$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$, and let $\zeta_A\in A$ be an element whose image in $L$ is $\zeta$. Let $K$ be any commutative $A$-algebra. The assertion is that there exists a type $A_0$ carrying the structure of a commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring, together with algebra structures $A_0\to A$ and $A_0\to K$ making $A_0$, $A$, $K$ a scalar tower, such that: the maximal ideal of $A_0$ is generated by $q$; the residue field of $A_0$ is finite; $A_0\to A$ is injective and $A$ is a finite $A_0$-module; the images of $\ell$, $M'$, $2$ and $3$ in $A_0$ are units; $A_0$ contains a primitive $\ell$-th root of unity; and the $A_0$-subalgebra of $A$ generated by $\zeta_A$ is all of $A$, i.e. $A=A_0[\zeta_A]$.
--
--   This is the number-field input behind the choice of a coefficient base: $A_0$ is the localisation of the ring of integers of $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_\ell)$ at the prime above $q$, so that $q$ is unramified there while $L/\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_\ell)$ is totally ramified at $q$, whence $A$ is generated over $A_0$ by $\zeta_q$. It provides the base ring together with its unit and root-of-unity data used by the full-level stalk and completion computations for the auxiliary level structures, which cite it to produce their $A_0$-frame.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_FullLevel_AuxLevel_exists_unramified_subDVR_adjoin_eq_top.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
